eq 0$. Then $|w|^2\geq |w(x)|^2/4$ on a ball $B(x,\delta )$ on which $u\leq M$ for some constant $M$, since $u$ is upper semicontinuous, and the last integral is at least \[   rac{|w(x)|^2}4\, \mathrm e^{-mM-\sup _{B_ l}|z|^2}\int _{B(x,\delta )}|z-x|^{-2n}\, \mathrm dV=\infty ,  \] because $\int _{B(x,\delta )}|z-x|^{-2n}\, \mathrm dV=s_{2n-1}\int _0^\delta t^{-1}\, \mathrm dt$ in polar coordinates ( 2.1 ). Hence $w(x)=0$. The function $F=ho f-w$ satisfies $ ar\partial F=0$ on $B_ l$, so it is holomorphic by theorem 2.1.4 , and $F(x)=f(x)$. Since $0\leq ho \leq 1$ and $ho $ vanishes outside $B_ j$, the weighted norm of $F$ on $B_ l$ is at most $1+C'^{1/2}=C_0$. By the definition of the kernel, $|f(x)|^2=|F(x)|^2\leq C_0^2K^{mu}_{B_ l}(x)$, and taking the supremum over $f$ gives $K^{mu}_{B_ j}(x)\leq C_0^2K^{mu}_{B_ l}(x)$. No bound on $u$, $	heta $ or $m$ enters these constants. Taking $C_ B$ to be the maximum of $2\log C_0$ over the finitely many pairs $(j,l)$, with the roles of $j$ and $l$ also exchanged, proves the lemma. $\square$ Theorem 13.3.3 . There is a constant $c>0$, depending only on $(X,\omega )$ through the fixed geometry, with the following property. Let $	heta $ be a smooth closed real $(1,1)$-form on $X$, let $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(X,	heta )$, and let $m\geq 1$ be an integer. Then there is a quasi-plurisubharmonic function $\varphi _ m$ on $X$ such that \[  	heta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}\varphi _ m\geq - rac cm\omega , \qquad \varphi _ m\geq  rac1m\log \sum _ i|g_ i|^2+O(1)\quad 	ext{locally},  \] where the $g_ i$ are local generators of $\mathcal I(m\varphi )$. The bounded term may depend on $m$, $	heta $, $\varphi $ and the point. Proof. By lemma 13.3.2 , the functions $v_{j,m}\in \mathrm{PSH}(B_ j,	heta )$ satisfy $v_{j,m}\leq v_{l,m}+C_ B/m$ on $\overline{U'_ j}\cap \overline{U'_ l}$, also at their common zeros. Hence lemma 13.3.1 with $a=C_ B/m$ gives a quasi-plurisubharmonic function $\varphi _ m$ with $	heta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}\