ot\in \overline{E}$; $x\in \overline{E}$ and there is an open neighborhood $U\subseteq \Omega $ of $x$ and $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(U)$ such that \[  \varlimsup _{(U\cap E)\setminus \{ x\}  
i y	o x} \varphi (y)<\varphi (x).  \] We say $E$ is thin if it is thin at all $x\in \Omega $. Remark 1.3.5 . In the second case, we can always arrange that \[  \varphi |_{(E\setminus \{ x\} )\cap U}=0.  \] In fact, we may assume that $\varphi \leq 0$ and $C<0$ is such that \[  \varlimsup _{(U\cap E)\setminus \{ x\}  
i y	o x} \varphi (y)<C<\varphi (x).  \] We let \[  \psi =(-C)^{-1}(\varphi \lor C)+1.  \] Then $\psi $ satisfies our requirements for a smaller $U$. In the second case, the function $\varphi $ can be very much improved. Proposition 1.3.6 (Bedford–Taylor) . Consider a set $E\subseteq \Omega $ and $x\in \overline{E}$. Assume that $E$ is thin at $x$. Then there is $\varphi \in \mathrm{PSH}(\mathbb {C}^ n)$ with the following properties: $\varphi $ is locally bounded outside $x$; $\varphi (x)>-\infty $; \[  \lim _{E\setminus \{ x\}  
