A.3 Classes of convex functions Let $N$ be a real vector space of finite dimension and $M$ be the dual vector space. We shall fix a convex body $P\subseteq M$. The following classes are introduced in [ BB13 ] . Definition A.3.1 . We define the set $\mathcal{P}(N,P)$ as the set of proper convex functions $F\in \mathrm{Conv}(N)$ such that $F\preceq \operatorname{Supp}_ P$. We define the set $\mathcal{E}^{\infty }(N,P)$ as the set of closed convex functions $F\in \mathrm{Conv}(N)$ such that $F\sim \operatorname{Supp}_ P$. We define the set $\mathcal{E}(N,P)$ as follows: Suppose that $\operatorname{Int}P=\varnothing $. Then $\mathcal{E}(N,P)\coloneqq \mathcal{P}(N,P)$; otherwise, let \[  \mathcal{E}(N,P)=\left\{ F\in \mathcal{P}(N,P) : P=\overline{
abla F(N)} ight\} .  \] We define the set $\mathcal{E}^1(N,P)$ as the subset of $\mathcal{E}(N,P)$ consisting of $F\in \mathcal{E}(N,P)$ with \[  \int _ P F^*\, \mathrm{d}\operatorname{vol}<\infty ,  \] where $\mathrm{d}\operatorname{vol}$ is any Lebesgue measure on $M$. Observe that for any $F\in \mathcal{P}(N,P)$, we have $\operatorname{Dom}F=N$ and $F$ is necessarily closed. Proposition A.3.2 . We have \[  \mathcal{E}^{\infty }(N,P)\subseteq \mathcal{E}^{1}(N,P)\subseteq \mathcal{E}(N,P) \subseteq \mathcal{P}(N,P).  \] Proof. When $\operatorname{Int}P=\varnothing $, the assertion is clear. We assume that $\operatorname{Int}P
eq \varnothing $. The second inclusion follows from the definition. We only handle the first inequality. Take $F\in \mathcal{E}^{\infty }(N,P)$. By definition, $F\sim \operatorname{Supp}_ P$ and hence $F^*\sim \chi _ P$. It follows that $P=\operatorname{Dom}F^*$. By proposition A.2.12 , we already know that \[  
abla F(N)\subseteq P=\operatorname{Dom}F^*.  \] On the other hand, by proposition A.2.9 , we have \[  \operatorname{Int}P\subseteq 
abla F(N).  \] So it follows that \[  P=\overline{
abla F(N)}.  \] It is clear that $F^*\sim \chi _ P$ is integrable. $\square$ Proposition A.3.3 . For any $F\in \mathcal{E}^{\infty }(N,P)$, we have $F^*|_{M\setminus P}\equiv \infty $ and $F^*$ is bounded on $P$. Proof. From $F\sim \operatorname{Supp}_ P$, we take the Legendre transform to get $F^*\sim \operatorname{Supp}_ P^*=\chi _ P$, where we applied example A.2.3 . $\square$ Definition A.3.4 . We endow the topology of pointwise convergence on $\mathcal{P}(N,P)$. Note that this topology coincides with the compact-open topology. See [ Theorem 10.8 , Roc70 ] . Proposition A.3.5 . Let $F\in \mathcal{P}(N,P)$. Then there is a decreasing sequence $F_ j\in \mathcal{E}^{\infty }(N,P)\cap C^{\infty }(N)$ converging to $F$. See [ Lemma 2.2 , BB13 ] . We observe that the point $0\in N$ plays a special role since it does in the definition of the support function. Proposition A.3.6 . For any $F\in \mathcal{P}(N,P)$, we have \[  \max _{N}(F-\operatorname{Supp}_ P)=F(0).  \] Proof. It follows from ( A.11 ) that \[  \sup _{N}(F-\operatorname{Supp}_ P)\leq F(0).  \] The equality is clearly obtained at $0\in N$. $\square$ Lemma A.3.7 . Let $P'\subseteq M$ be another convex body. Then for any $F\in \mathcal{P}(N,P)$ and $F'\in \mathcal{P}(N,P')$, we have \[  F+F'\in \mathcal{P}(N,P+P').  \] Moreover, let $F$ and $F'$ be real-valued convex functions on $N$. Assume that $\operatorname{Dom}F^*$ is bounded. Then \[  \overline{
abla F(N)}+\overline{
abla F'(N)}=\overline{
abla (F+F')(N)}.  \] In particular, if $F\in \mathcal{P}(N,P)$, $F'\in \mathcal{P}(N,P')$ and \[  \overline{
abla F(N)}=P,\quad \overline{
