4.11 Conformal changes and directional curvature positivity The compatibility condition of proposition 4.8.1 decides which right-hand sides a scalar trace equation can have, and it can be used to improve the curvature of a Hermitian metric on a vector bundle. A conformal change $h\mapsto \mathrm e^{-tf}h$ adds the same form $t\, \mathrm i\partial  ar\partial f$ to the curvature in every fiber direction. Solving a Poisson equation for a Gauduchon form, we can make the trace of this form positive outside any prescribed ball. If the original curvature already has a positive direction for every fiber vector over that ball, a small conformal change produces such a direction everywhere. Throughout this section, $E	o X$ is a holomorphic vector bundle of positive rank, and $n\geq 2$. For a smooth Hermitian metric $h$ on $E$, we use the Chern curvature $F_ h$, the Hermitian endomorphisms $R_ h(u, ar u)$, the directional forms $\alpha _{h,v}$ and the mean curvature $K_{\omega ,h}$ of 4.6.9 ; recall that $h$ is linear in its first argument. Definition 4.11.1 . The metric $h$ is RC-positive at $x$ if, for every $0
e v\in E_ x$, there is a tangent vector $u\in T_ x^{1,0}X$ such that \[  h(R_ h(u, ar u)v,v)>0.  \] It is uniformly RC-positive at $x$ if one tangent vector $u$ works for every nonzero $v\in E_ x$. A metric has either property on a set if it has that property at every point of the set. Lemma 4.11.2 . Suppose that $K_{\omega ,h}(x)\geq 0$. Then $h$ is RC-positive at $x$ if and only if $\alpha _{h,v}
e 0$ for every $0
