e 0$, so it has a positive eigenvalue there. Over $Q$, the unit sphere bundle $\{ |v|_ h=1\} $ is compact. The largest $\gamma $-eigenvalue of $\alpha _{h,v}$ is continuous and strictly positive on it, so it has a positive minimum $\delta $. Let $C$ be the maximum on $X$ of the operator norm of $\mathrm i\partial  ar\partial f$ relative to $\gamma $. For every unit vector over $Q$, the largest eigenvalue of the expression in parentheses in ( 4.31 ) is at least $\delta -tC$, which is positive for small $t>0$. Homogeneity in $v$ and the positive factor $\mathrm e^{-tf}$ prove the assertion on $Q$ as well. $\square$ In this conclusion, the positive direction may depend on the fiber vector $v$. The distinction from uniform RC-positivity already occurs for a local metric with zero mean curvature. Example 4.11.4 . On a sufficiently small ball in $\mathbb C^2$, equip the trivial bundle of rank two with the metric \[  H(z)=\operatorname {Id}-zz^*+	frac 12|z|^2\operatorname {Id}.  \] At the origin this metric has zero mean curvature for the Euclidean Hermitian form and is RC-positive, but it is not uniformly RC-positive. Proof. The two eigenvalues of $H(z)$ are $1-	frac 12|z|^2$ and $1+	frac 12|z|^2$, so the metric is positive on a small ball. Its first derivatives vanish at the origin, where \[  F_{i ar j}=e_ ie_ j^*-	frac 12\delta _{ij}\operatorname {Id},\qquad h(R_ h(u, ar u)v,v)=|v^*u|^2-	frac 12|u|^2|v|^2.  \] The sum $F_{1 ar1}+F_{2 ar2}$ is zero. For each nonzero $v$, taking $u=v$ gives a positive evaluation. For each nonzero $u$, taking $v\perp u$ gives a negative evaluation, so no single $u$ works for every $v$. $\square$ Proposition 4.11.5 . If $\det E$ admits a flat Hermitian metric, then no smooth Hermitian metric on $E$ is uniformly RC-positive on $X$ or has positive definite mean curvature relative to a Hermitian form. This applies in particular when $\det E$ is holomorphically trivial. Proof. If $h$ were uniformly RC-positive, then at each point some $u$ would