8.2 The radial–vertical block We look for potentials that depend on the base point only through its distance from the origin of the affine chart. In a suitable frame, the matrix of $\omega +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}V$ then consists of one tangential horizontal coefficient and a two-by-two radial–vertical block, whose Schur complement isolates the mixed derivative that must be controlled. Subscripts denote partial derivatives in the indicated variables. For $\xi \ne 0$ introduce \[  x=\log |\xi |^2,\qquad \mu =\frac{\mathrm{e}^ x}{1+\mathrm{e}^ x},\qquad \mu _ x=\mu (1-\mu ).  \] We use smooth real functions $V:\mathbb R\times E\to \mathbb R$, written $V=V(x,w)$, that are independent of $x$ for $x$ near $-\infty $ and constant in both variables for $x$ near $+\infty $. These conditions allow $V$ to be extended smoothly across $\xi =0$, and over the complement of the affine chart by the same constant. The region of the base where $V$ is nonconstant may depend on $V$. Lemma 8.2.1 . For such a function, at $\xi \ne 0$ the inequality $\omega +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}V>0$ is equivalent to 8.5 \begin{equation} \label{eq:hopf-positivity} 1+V_{w\bar w}>0,\qquad \mu +V_ x>0,\qquad Q[V]:=V_{xx}+\mu - \frac{|\mu +V_{x\bar w}|^2}{1+V_{w\bar w}}>0. \end{equation} At $\xi =0$, the inequality $\omega +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}V>0$ is equivalent to $1+V_{w\bar w}>0$. Proof. In these coordinates, \[  s=w+\bar w+\log (1+\mathrm{e}^ x),\qquad \partial s=\mathrm{d}w+\mu \partial x,  \] and \[  2\pi \alpha =\mathrm i\mu \partial \bar\partial x +\mathrm i\mu (1-\mu )\partial x\wedge \bar\partial x.  \] Choose horizontal vectors of unit length for $2\pi \alpha $, one orthogonal to the radial direction and the other \[  e_ r=\frac{\xi _1\partial _{\xi _1}+\xi _2\partial _{\xi _2}}{\sqrt{\mu (1-\mu )}}.  \] Adjoin the vertical vector $\partial _ w$. In this frame the matrix of $2\pi (\omega +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}V)$ is \[  \begin{pmatrix}  (\mu +V_ x)/\mu 

& 0

& 0

\\  0

& (\mu +V_{xx})/[\mu (1-\mu )]

&  (\mu +V_{x\bar w})/\sqrt{\mu (1-\mu )}

\\  0

& (\mu +V_{wx})/\sqrt{\mu (1-\mu )}

& 1+V_{w\bar w} 

\end{pmatrix}.  \] Taking the Schur complement of the last diagonal entry gives ( 8.5 ). At $\xi =0$, the derivatives of $V$ in the base directions vanish and $\partial s=\mathrm{d}w$, which gives the last assertion. $\square$ A rapid cutoff of a concentrated fiber potential can make the mixed derivative $V_{x\bar w}$ too large for this criterion. We therefore spread the concentration by the heat equation before cutting off, so that the fiber derivatives are already exponentially small in the cutoff region.

