8.6 Higher-dimensional Hopf manifolds The threefold mass formula locates the source of unbounded mass, but it does not by itself give a construction in higher dimension. The same heat potentials do work there: the radial–vertical block is unchanged, and each extra angular direction contributes a factor whose lower bound is uniform where the threefold mass diverges. For $n\geq 3$, let \[  H_ n=(\mathbb C^ n\setminus \{ 0\} )/ \langle z\mapsto \mathrm e^{-1}z\rangle , \qquad p_ n:H_ n\longrightarrow \mathbb P^{n-1}.  \] Put $s=\log |z|^2$, $\alpha _ n=\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}s=p_ n^*\omega _{\mathrm{FS}}$, $\beta _ n=\mathrm ds\wedge \mathrm d^{\mathrm c}s$ and $\omega _ n=\alpha _ n+\beta _ n$, where $\int _{\mathbb P^{n-1}}\omega _{\mathrm{FS}}^{n-1}=1$. The forms descend and $\omega _ n$ is positive for the same reasons as in dimension three. The fiber is the same elliptic curve $E$, with $\int _ E\beta _ n=2$. Since $\alpha _ n$ has rank $n-1$ and $\beta _ n^2=0$, \[  \int _{H_ n}\omega _ n^ n=2n,\qquad \mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}(\omega _ n^{n-1})=-(n-1)\alpha _ n^ n=0.  \] Thus $\omega _ n$ is Gauduchon. On the affine chart write $\xi \in \mathbb C^{n-1}$, $x=\log |\xi |^2$, $w=\log z_0\pmod\Lambda $ and $\mu =\mathrm e^ x/(1+\mathrm e^ x)$ as before. For a smooth real function $V(x,w)$ with the endpoint properties of lemma 8.2.1 , let $J_ n(V;x,w)$ be the density obtained by integrating $(\omega _ n+\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}V)^ n$ over the angular variables, with angular probability measure. Thus \[  \int _{H_ n}(\omega _ n+\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}V)^ n =\int _{\mathbb R\times E}J_ n(V;x,w)\, \mathrm dx\, \mathrm dA_ E.  \] The endpoints and the complement of the affine chart have zero smooth volume. Lemma 8.6.1 . The positivity criterion ( 8.5 ) holds in every dimension $n\geq 3$, and 8.24 \begin{equation} \label{eq:hopf-dimensional-density} J_ n(V;x,w)=\frac{n(n-1)}6 (\mu +V_ x)^{n-3}J_3(V;x,w). \end{equation} Here $J_3$ is evaluated for the same function $V$ on the threefold. Proof. Choose $n-2$ horizontal angular vectors, one horizontal radial vector and the vertical vector $\partial _ w$. In the normalization of the proof of lemma 8.2.1 , each angular entry is $A=(\mu +V_ x)/\mu $, and the radial–vertical block is \[  K_ V=\begin{pmatrix}  (\mu +V_{xx})/[\mu (1-\mu )]

&  (\mu +V_{x\bar w})/\sqrt{\mu (1-\mu )}

\\  (\mu +V_{wx})/\sqrt{\mu (1-\mu )}

& 1+V_{w\bar w} 

\end{pmatrix}.  \] This follows by differentiating $s=w+\bar w+\log (1+\mathrm e^ x)$: the radial part of $\partial \bar\partial x$ is zero, whereas its angular part contributes $V_ x/\mu $. The Schur complement of the last entry gives the stated positivity criterion. At $\xi =0$ the same endpoint argument as in dimension three applies. Set $d=n-1$. The radial Fubini–Study probability density is $d\mu ^{d-1}\mathrm d\mu =d\mu ^ d(1-\mu )\mathrm dx$; it follows from the polar-coordinate determinant of $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}\log (1+|\xi |^2)$ and its integral is one. The background block $K_0$ has determinant one. Using the fiber mass two, therefore, \[  J_ n(V)=2nd\mu ^ d(1-\mu )A^{n-2}\det K_ V.  \] For $n=3$ this is $12\mu ^2(1-\mu )A\det K_ V$. Comparing the two expressions proves ( 8.24 ). $\square$ Theorem 8.6.2 . For every $n\geq 3$, the Gauduchon metric $\omega _ n$ admits smooth potentials $V_\eta $ with $\omega _ n+\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}V_\eta >0$ whose total Monge–Ampère masses tend to infinity as $\eta \downarrow 0$. Proof. Use exactly the functions $V_\eta =W+g_\eta $ constructed in the proof of theorem 8.0.1 , including the heat scale $\varepsilon =\eta ^{1/\eta ^2}$. Their endpoint properties give smooth potentials on every $H_ n$, and lemma 8.6.1 gives strict positivity. The slope bounds in lemma 8.4.1 and $-m_\eta \leq g_\eta '\leq 0$ imply \[  \mu +(V_\eta )_ x\geq \tfrac 14 \qquad (x\geq 0)  \] for all sufficiently small $\eta $. We must first localize the divergent threefold mass to this region. On $x<0$, the heat potential is $W=F_\eta (\varepsilon ,w)$ and has no $x$ derivatives. The determinant formula in the preceding proof and $\int _ E F_{w\bar w}\, \mathrm dA_ E=0$ give \[  \int _ E J_3(V_\eta ;x,w)\, \mathrm dA_ E =12(\mu +g_\eta ') \bigl (g_\eta ''+\mu (1-\mu )\bigr ).  \] Here $0<\mu +g_\eta '\leq \mu $, and $g_\eta ''=r_\eta -m_\eta \psi $ with $\int r_\eta =m_\eta $ and $\int \psi =1$. Hence \[  \int _{x<0}\! J_3(V_\eta )\, \mathrm