}-C\varepsilon \gamma ^{n-1}$. Let $Q_ u$ be the coefficient matrix of $\Gamma _ u$ in a fixed local frame of $(n-1,0)$-forms. Since $\gamma _0^{n-1}$ is strictly positive, choosing $\varepsilon $ small gives \[  Q_ u\geq cI  \] with a uniform $c>0$. The equation and the bounds for $F+b$ already proved bound $\det Q_ u$ from above, because the determinant of this matrix is, up to a fixed positive factor depending on the frame, the $(n-1)$st power of the density of $\eta _ u^ n$. All eigenvalues of $Q_ u$ are therefore bounded above as well: their product is bounded, and each of them is at least $c$. In particular, $\gamma \wedge \Gamma _ u/\gamma ^ n$ is uniformly bounded on $A$. Subtracting its fixed background term and its bounded first-order term, we obtain an upper bound for \[   rac{\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}u\wedge \gamma ^{n-1}}{\gamma ^ n}.  \] This is a fixed positive multiple of the $\gamma $-trace of the complex Hessian of $u$, which differs from that of $v$ by a bounded term. The complex Hessian of $v$ is nonnegative on $A$, since the real Hessian is nonnegative there, so comparison of metrics bounds its Euclidean trace from above. The real trace of $D^2v$ is four times this complex trace. It follows that \[  0\leq \det D^2v \leq \left( rac{\operatorname {tr}_{\mathbb R}D^2v}{2n}ight)^{2n} \leq C_2\quad \hbox{on }A.  \] Inequality ( 9.22 ) now gives $|A|\geq c_2>0$, where $c_2$ is independent of $u$. On $A$ we also have $u\leq v\leq M+\varepsilon /2$. Consequently the uniform $L^1$ bound gives \[  C_3\geq \int _ A(-u)\, \mathrm{d}x \geq c_2(-M-\varepsilon /2).  \] This bounds $-M$ and completes the proof. $\square$

9.12 The matrix structure of the Gauduchon equation The Hodge star converts the positive $(n-1,n-1)$-form $\Gamma _ u$ into a Hermitian matrix whose logarithmic determinant is a concave function of the complex Hessian. Two features will control the largest eigenvalue in the second-order estimate: the positive background gives coercivity, and the torsion coefficients omit exactly those gradient indices that would otherwise carry the largest inverse weight. We derive both properties before differentiating the equation. For the matrix and derivative calculations, set $v=u/(2\pi )$, so that $\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}u=\mathrm i\partial \bar\partial v$. All covariant derivatives are taken with respect to the Chern connection $\nabla $ of $\gamma $. In holomorphic coordinates $z^1,\ldots ,z^ n$, write $v_ i=\partial _ i v$ and $v_{\bar j}=\partial _{\bar j}v$; indices range from $1$ to $n$, and $\delta _{ij}$ is the Kronecker symbol. Write $\gamma =\mathrm i\gamma _{a\bar b}\, \mathrm{d}z^ a\wedge \mathrm{d}\bar z^ b$, with repeated indices summed. We denote by $\star _\gamma $ the Hodge star of $\gamma $, and by $\det _\gamma $ the determinant of the Hermitian matrix of a real $(1,1)$-form relative to $\gamma $. Write the torsion of $\nabla $ as $T_{ab}^{c}$ and lower the last index with $\gamma $. Thus \[  T_{ab\bar c}=\partial _ a\gamma _{b\bar c}-\partial _ b\gamma _{a\bar c}, \qquad T_{ab\bar c}=-T_{ba\bar c}.  \] Let $Q_0=\star _\gamma \gamma _0^{n-1}/(n-1)!$ and \[  Z=\frac1{(n-1)!}\star _\gamma \operatorname {Re}\bigl (\mathrm i\partial v\wedge \bar\partial \gamma ^{n-2}\bigr ).  \] Here the real $(1,1)$-forms on the right are identified with their Hermitian coefficient matrices relative to $\gamma $. Set \[  \chi =(\operatorname {tr}_\gamma Q_0)\gamma -(n-1)Q_0, \quad W=(\operatorname {tr}_\gamma Z)\gamma -(n-1)Z, \quad g=\chi +\mathrm i\partial \bar\partial v+W.  \] The linear map $P_\gamma (A)=((\operatorname {tr}_\gamma A)\gamma -A)/(n-1)$ on real $(1,1)$-forms