\end{pmatrix}.  \] Let $A=\star _\gamma (\mathrm i\partial v\wedge \bar\partial \gamma ^{n-2})/(n-1)!$. Expanding the $n-3$ remaining copies of $\gamma $ leaves the three complementary indices and gives \[  A_{i\bar j}=\frac1{2(n-1)} \sum _{p,a,b,c}v_ p\overline{T_{bc\bar a}}\,  \delta ^{paj}_{bci}.  \] Indeed, the factor is $(n-2)(n-3)!/[2(n-1)!]$, and the determinant records the signs of the remaining holomorphic and antiholomorphic indices. Expanding its six terms and using $T_{bc\bar a}=-T_{cb\bar a}$, we obtain \[  A_{i\bar j}=\frac1{n-1}\left( \delta _{ij}\sum _{p,k}v_ p\overline{T_{pk\bar k}} -v_ i\sum _ k\overline{T_{jk\bar k}} -\sum _ kv_ k\overline{T_{kj\bar i}}\right).  \] Since $Z=(A+A^*)/2$, where $A^*$ denotes the Hermitian adjoint matrix, this gives ( 9.29 ). For $i\ne j$, the coefficient of $v_ j$ in this expression vanishes because $T_{jj\bar i}=0$, and the coefficient of $v_{\bar i}$ vanishes in the conjugate expression. For $i=j$, canceling the terms with $p=i$ and $k=i$ gives ( 9.30 ). These are tensor identities valid in every unitary frame. Since the Chern connection preserves the metric, they can be differentiated in a unitary frame that is parallel at the point. For instance, the first derivative of a summand in ( 9.30 ) is \[  (\nabla _1v_ p)\overline{T_{pk\bar k}} +v_ p\nabla _1\overline{T_{pk\bar k}},\qquad p,k\ne 1,  \] together with its conjugate counterpart. A further derivative in the direction $\bar1$ still has $p\ne 1$. This gives the stated exclusions, including the terms in which a derivative falls on a coefficient. $\square$ The second-order estimate uses these exclusions; an arbitrary first-order perturbation could not be substituted for $Z$. Lemma 9.12.2 . Diagonalize $g$ at a point and order its eigenvalues as $\lambda _1\geq \cdots \geq \lambda _ n$. Put \[  \mu _ i=\frac{\sum _{j\ne i}\lambda _ j}{n-1}>0, \quad t_ i=\mu _ i^{-1},\quad f_ i=\frac1{n-1}\sum _{j\ne i}t_ j, \quad \mathcal F=\sum _ i f_ i=\sum _ i t_ i.  \] Then $