u =\mathrm{MA}_	heta (\psi )$, a finite non-pluripolar measure. Step 1: smooth strictly positive density. First suppose $f$ is smooth and strictly positive. Choose a Hermitian form $\Omega \geq 	heta $ and, by theorem 1.11.1 , smooth $\Omega $-plurisubharmonic functions $\psi _ j\downarrow \psi $. The envelopes \[  q_ j=P_	heta (\psi _ j)  \] lie between $\psi $ and $\psi _ j$, so they are uniformly bounded and decrease to $\psi $. Contact support and ordered contact in the background $\Omega $ give \[  \mathrm{MA}_	heta (q_ j) \leq \mathbf1_{\{ q_ j=\psi _ j\} }\Omega _{\psi _ j}^ n.  \] In particular, each $\mathrm{MA}_	heta (q_ j)$ has a bounded density with respect to smooth volume. Moreover $\mathrm{MA}_	heta (q_ j)(X)>0$ by proposition 13.2.1 . Theorem 14.2.2 therefore gives bounded $u_ j\in \mathrm{PSH}(X,	heta )$ with \[  \mathrm{MA}_	heta (u_ j)=\mathrm{e}^{\lambda u_ j}f\,  \mathrm{MA}_	heta (q_ j).  \] Since $f$ is bounded above and away from zero, we can choose $C$ such that $\mathrm{e}^{\lambda (q_ j-C)}f\leq 1\leq \mathrm{e}^{\lambda (q_ j+C)}f$ for all $j$. Then $q_ j-C$ and $q_ j+C$ are a subsolution and a supersolution, and bounded exponential comparison gives $q_ j-C\leq u_ j\leq q_ j+C$. Extract an $L^1$ limit $u$, which is again bounded. The moving domination \[  \mathrm{MA}_	heta (u_ j)\leq C'\Omega _{\psi _ j}^ n  \] and corollary 14.8.3 , applied with the reference zero, give convergence in capacity. Continuity of bounded products under convergence in capacity, with the quasi-continuous weights $\mathrm{e}^{\lambda u_ j}f$, then gives \[  \mathrm{MA}_	heta (u)=\mathrm{e}^{\lambda u}f
u .  \] Step 2: bounded measurable density. Choose smooth positive functions $f_ j$ with a common upper bound $L>0$ such that $f_ j	o f$ in $L^1(
u )$. Such an approximation exists: by regularity of the finite measure $
