15.2 Supporting probability measures A supporting functional of the concave function $B_\varphi $ describes the first-order response of the scalar to a change of logarithmic density. Monotonicity and the translation rule force such a functional to be minus the integral against a probability measure. We work first on the space $C(X)$ of continuous real functions, with the uniform norm. For $F\in C(X)$, let $\mathcal A_\varphi (F)$ be the set of Borel probability measures $
u $ on $X$ satisfying 15.5  egin{equation} \label{eq:adjoint-probability-set} B_\varphi (G)\leq B_\varphi (F)-\int _ X(G-F)\, \mathrm{d}
u \qquad 	ext{for every }G\in C(X). \end{equation} Thus $G\mapsto -\int _ XG\, \mathrm{d}
u $ is a supergradient of $B_\varphi $ at $F$. We call the members of $\mathcal A_\varphi (F)$ the supporting probabilities at $F$. Theorem 15.2.1 . For every $F\in C(X)$, the set $\mathcal A_\varphi (F)$ is nonempty, convex and weakly compact. Every one of its measures is absolutely continuous with respect to $\mathrm{d}V$. Proof. The function $C_\varphi =-B_\varphi $ is convex and Lipschitz. Fix $F\in C(X)$. For a direction $h\in C(X)$, the limit \[  p(h)=\lim _{t\downarrow 0}  rac{C_\varphi (F+th)-C_\varphi (F)}{t}  \] exists and is finite: convexity makes the difference quotients monotone in $t>0$, and their absolute values are at most $\| h\| _\infty $. Clearly $p$ is positively homogeneous. Given another direction $k\in C(X)$, apply convexity at the midpoint of $F+2th$ and $F+2tk$ and let $t\downarrow 0$; this gives $p(h+k)\leq p(h)+p(k)$. Thus $p$ is sublinear. By the Hahn–Banach theorem, the zero functional on $\{ 0\} $ extends to a linear functional $\ell \leq p$. Since $p(h)\leq \| h\| _\infty $, applying this bound to $h$ and $-h$ shows that $\ell $ is continuous. Monotonicity of the convex difference quotients gives \[  C_\varphi (F+h)\geq C_\varphi (F)+\ell (h),  \] which is ( 15.5 ) once $\ell $ is represented by a probability. Testing the translation rule with positive and negative constants gives $\ell (1)=1$. If $h\geq 0$, monotonicity of $B_\varphi $ gives $C_\varphi (F-th)\leq C_\varphi (F)$ for $t>0$, whereas the inequality just proved gives the lower bound $C_\varphi (F)-t\ell (h)$. Hence $\ell (h)\geq 0$. By the Riesz representation theorem, $\ell (h)=\int _ Xh\, \mathrm{d}
u $ for a probability measure $
u $, and $
u \in \mathcal A_\varphi (F)$. Each inequality in ( 15.5 ) defines a weakly closed half-space in the compact space of probability measures, so their intersection is closed and convex, hence weakly compact. It remains to prove absolute continuity. Let $K$ be compact with $\mathrm{d}V(K)=0$, and choose continuous functions $0\leq \chi _ j\leq 1$, equal to one on $K$, whose supports have volumes tending to zero. For any $
u \in \mathcal A_\varphi (F)$, the supporting inequality with $G=F+\chi _ j$ gives \[  0\leq 
u (K)\leq \int _ X\chi _ j\, \mathrm{d}
u \leq B_\varphi (F)-B_\varphi (F+\chi _ j).  \] The right-hand side tends to zero by theorem 15.1.3 , so $
u (K)=0$. By inner regularity of finite Borel measures on $X$, the same holds for every Borel set of zero volume. The Radon–Nikodym theorem now gives $\mathrm{d}
u =h_
u \mathrm{d}V$ with $h_
u \geq 0$ and $\int _ Xh_
u \mathrm{d}V=1$. $\square$ Proposition 15.2.2 . Let $F_ j,F\in C(X)$ with $F_ j	o F$ uniformly, and let $
u _ j\in \mathcal A_\varphi (F_ j)$. Then every weak cluster point of $(
u _ j)$ belongs to $\mathcal A_\varphi (F)$. In particular, if the latter set consists of a single measure, $
u _ j$ converges weakly to it. Proof. Take a weakly convergent subsequence and fix $G\in C(X)$. In the inequality \[  B_\varphi (G)\leq B_\varphi (F_ j)-\int _ X(G-F_ j)\, \mathrm{d}
u _ j,  \] the Lipschitz continuity of $B_\varphi $, the uniform convergence of $F_ j$ and the weak convergence of the probabilities allow passage to the limit. The limiting inequality holds for every $G$, so the limit measure belongs to $\mathcal A_\varphi (F)$. If that set is a singleton, every convergent subsequence has the same limit, and compactness of the space of probability measures proves the final assertion. $\square$

