u \geq 0.  \] The extrema exist by weak compactness. We claim that the width is upper semicontinuous in $F$. Indeed, let $F_ j	o F$ and choose maximizing and minimizing probabilities at each $F_ j$. Along a subsequence realizing the upper limit of the widths, compactness of the space of probability measures gives weak limits of both choices. By proposition 15.2.2 , both limits belong to $\mathcal A_\varphi (F)$, so the difference of their integrals against $h$ is at most $w_ h(F)$. Consequently, for every $\epsilon >0$ the set $\{ F:w_ h(F)<\epsilon \} $ is open. It is also dense. If $h=0$ there is nothing to prove. Otherwise fix $F$ and an open ball about it. The function \[  b(t)=B_\varphi (F+th)  \] is finite and concave on the real line, so it is differentiable outside an at most countable set: its one-sided derivatives are finite and monotone, and the nonempty open intervals between unequal one-sided derivatives at distinct points are disjoint, so each contains a different rational number. Choose a point of differentiability $t$ so close to zero that $F+th$ lies in the given ball. The derivative formulas of proposition 15.2.3 give $w_ h(F+th)=0$, which proves density. The compact manifold $X$ is metrizable, so $C(X)$ is separable. Choose a countable uniformly dense family $(h_ j)$ in $C(X)$. By the Baire category theorem in the complete metric space $C(X)$, the set \[   igcap _{j,k\geq 1}\{ F:w_{h_ j}(F)<1/k\}   \] is a dense $G_\delta $. At every point of this intersection, all supporting probabilities have the same integral against every $h_ j$. Since they have unit mass, uniform approximation gives equal integrals against every continuous function, and the Riesz representation theorem shows that the probabilities coincide. Conversely, a singleton has zero width in every direction, so the intersection is exactly $\mathscr U_\varphi $. The formula for the derivative follows from proposition 15.2.3 , and the approximation assertion from proposition 15.2.2 . $\s