15.8 Varying positive backgrounds A two-sided inequality between two Hermitian forms compares their normalization scalars uniformly in the logarithmic density. This turns geometric convergence of backgrounds into the uniform comparison of functionals used above. From this section on, each section specifies its own background and reference. Here we vary a Hermitian form $ eta $ on $X$ and use the zero reference. Write $B_ eta $, $H_ eta $ and $\mathcal A_ eta $ for the normalization scalar, its dual functional and the supporting sets, defined as above with $	heta = eta $ and $\varphi =0$. For two such backgrounds, set \[  d(B_ eta ,B_{ eta '})=\sup _{F\in L^\infty (\mathrm{d}V)}|B_ eta (F)-B_{ eta '}(F)|.  \] The supremum ranges over all bounded measurable tests, with no common bound on their norms. Theorem 15.8.1 . Suppose $0<\varepsilon <1$ and $(1-\varepsilon ) eta \leq  eta '\leq (1+\varepsilon ) eta $. For every bounded measurable $F$, 15.29  egin{equation} \label{eq:geometric-normalization-comparison} n\log (1-\varepsilon )\leq B_{ eta '}(F)-B_ eta (F)\leq n\log (1+\varepsilon ). \end{equation} In particular, $d(B_ eta ,B_{ eta '})\leq -n\log (1-\varepsilon )$. The same conclusion holds if the two inequalities between forms are satisfied by positive representatives $ eta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}h$ and $ eta '+\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}h'$, with $h,h'$ smooth real functions. Proof. First let $F$ be smooth, and let $v,v'$ be smooth solutions in the two backgrounds. At a maximum point of $v'-(1+\varepsilon )v$, \[  0< eta '+\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}v'\leq (1+\varepsilon )( eta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}v).  \] Taking determinants and canceling $\mathrm{e}^ F\mathrm{d}V$ gives the upper bound. At a minimum point of $v'-(1-\varepsilon )v$, the reverse inequality between forms gives the lower bound. For bounded measurable $F$, choose smooth uniformly bounded approximations converging in $L^1$ and apply theorem 15.1.3 in each of the two backgrounds. Finally, the correspondence $v\mapsto v-h$ preserves both boundedness and the current, so $B_{ eta +\mathrm{dd}^{\mathrm{c}}h}=B_ eta $ whenever both backgrounds are positive. This proves the assertion about representatives. $\square$ Corollary 15.8.2 . Let $ eta _ j$ be Hermitian forms with $(1-\varepsilon _ j) eta \leq  eta _ j\leq (1+\varepsilon _ j) eta $, where $0<\varepsilon _ j<1$ and $\varepsilon _ j	o 0$. Suppose that $F_ j	o F$ in $L^1(\mathrm{d}V)$ with a common $L^\infty $ bound. Then $B_{ eta _ j}(F_ j)	o B_ eta (F)$. For any $
u _ j\in \mathcal A_{ eta _ j}(F_ j)$, the densities are uniformly integrable, and every subsequence has a further subsequence converging weakly in $L^1$ to an element of $\mathcal A_ eta (F)$. If this set is a singleton, which is the case in particular when $F$ is smooth, the whole sequence converges to it weakly in $L^1$ and as probability measures. Proof. Put $\delta _ j=-n\log (1-\varepsilon _ j)$. The scalar assertion follows from ( 15.29 ) and theorem 15.1.3 in the fixed background $ eta $. Taking suprema over tests gives the inequalities, valid also for infinite values, \[  H_{ eta _ j}\leq H_ eta +\delta _ j,\qquad H_ eta \leq H_{ eta _ j}+\delta _ j.  \] In particular, the two duals are finite on the same measures, and the identity $H_{ eta _ j}(
u _ j)=B_{ eta _ j}(F_ j)+\int _ XF_ j\, \mathrm{d}
u _ j$ gives \[  0\leq H_ eta (
u _ j)-B_ eta (F_ j)-\int _ XF_ j\, \mathrm{d}
u _ j\leq 2\delta _ j.  \] If $\| F_ j\| _\infty \leq M$, this puts the measures in the fixed sublevel set $\{ H_ eta \leq B_ eta (0)+2M+2\sup _ j\delta _ j\} $, to which theorem 15.3.2 applies. For a weak $L^1$ cluster point, uniform integrability gives $\int _ X|F_ j-F|\, \mathrm{d}
