u \| _{\mathrm{TV}} =\lambda \| \mu -
u \| _{\mathrm{TV}}.  \] The volume identity follows from $A_1-\lambda \mu (Y)=\lambda (A_0-\mu (Y))$. Taking the atomic part commutes with multiplication by $\lambda $, which gives the ideal-envelope identity. The area ratios multiply, proving the composition rule. $\square$ Thus, for $A_\sigma >0$, the rescaled distances $d_\sigma /A_\sigma $ make all these model spaces isometric. The unscaled spaces have diameter $2A_\sigma $: this is an upper bound for the total variation of the difference of two positive measures of mass at most $A_\sigma $, and the models of $A_\sigma \delta _ x$ and $A_\sigma \delta _ y$, $x
